WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.670
This chapter puts four scores side by side, because they are easy to mix up.

2
00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:11.704
The Apgar score rates a newborn's condition right after birth.

3
00:00:11.704 --> 00:00:15.926
The Glasgow Coma Scale rates level of consciousness.

4
00:00:15.926 --> 00:00:20.472
The Ballard score estimates a newborn's gestational age.

5
00:00:20.472 --> 00:00:26.643
And the Silverman score rates how much respiratory distress an infant is in.

6
00:00:26.643 --> 00:00:34.599
For each one we cover what it measures, who it is used on, its range, and which direction is good.

7
00:00:34.599 --> 00:00:41.825
We spend the most time on the Glasgow Coma Scale, then finish with Ballard and Silverman.

8
00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:47.307
Start with what each score measures, and on whom.

9
00:00:47.307 --> 00:01:00.112
The Apgar assesses a newborn's condition right after birth, at one and five minutes; Hess adds a ten minute score if the five minute score is below seven.

10
00:01:00.112 --> 00:01:14.745
The Glasgow Coma Scale assesses level of consciousness in patients with altered consciousness or brain injury; Hess places it in the D, or deficits, step of the primary survey.

11
00:01:14.745 --> 00:01:25.887
The Ballard score estimates gestational age in newborns, especially premature ones, and Hess says to assess it by twelve hours of age.

12
00:01:25.887 --> 00:01:34.783
The Silverman score measures the magnitude of respiratory distress in infants; Hess gives no timing for it.

13
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.472
Now the ranges.

14
00:01:37.472 --> 00:01:50.535
The Apgar scores appearance, pulse, grimace, activity and respirations, zero to two each, for zero to ten, and higher is better, with seven to ten normal.

15
00:01:50.535 --> 00:02:03.343
The Glasgow scale adds eyes, up to four, verbal, up to five, and motor, up to six, for three to fifteen; higher is better, and eight or less is severe.

16
00:02:03.343 --> 00:02:07.499
The Ballard total converts to weeks of gestation.

17
00:02:07.499 --> 00:02:16.745
On the New Ballard, forty equals forty weeks, so higher means an older gestational age, not a healthier baby.

18
00:02:16.745 --> 00:02:24.633
The Silverman grades five signs, zero to two each, for zero to ten, and here higher is worse.

19
00:02:24.633 --> 00:02:30.825
That is the trap: Silverman runs the opposite way from Apgar and Glasgow.

20
00:02:32.233 --> 00:02:33.679
Pause and decide.

21
00:02:33.679 --> 00:02:40.995
An infant has an Apgar score of eight at five minutes, and a Silverman score of eight.

22
00:02:40.995 --> 00:02:45.333
Which number is reassuring, and which is a warning?

23
00:02:46.233 --> 00:02:52.233
[6-second practice pause]

24
00:02:52.733 --> 00:02:55.529
The Apgar of eight is reassuring.

25
00:02:55.529 --> 00:03:00.611
Apgar runs higher equals better, and seven to ten is normal.

26
00:03:00.611 --> 00:03:03.661
The Silverman of eight is a warning.

27
00:03:03.661 --> 00:03:16.367
Silverman runs the other way, so higher means more distress, and Heuer's Silverman-Anderson scale calls seven or higher impending respiratory failure.

28
00:03:16.367 --> 00:03:18.908
Same number, opposite meaning.

29
00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:27.723
The Glasgow Coma Scale provides the most objective assessment of consciousness.

30
00:03:27.723 --> 00:03:34.831
Add three parts: eyes, up to four; verbal, up to five; and motor, up to six.

31
00:03:34.831 --> 00:03:38.292
The maximum, fifteen, is fully alert.

32
00:03:38.292 --> 00:03:45.027
The minimum, three, is deep coma, and Hess says it suggests brain death.

33
00:03:45.027 --> 00:03:55.315
The lower the score, the worse the injury, and the motor response is the best predictor of neurologic outcome.

34
00:03:55.315 --> 00:04:06.353
RCP 203 bands the total: thirteen to fifteen is mild, nine to twelve moderate, and eight or less severe, meaning coma.

35
00:04:06.353 --> 00:04:21.973
The RCP 105 slide text prints mild as thirteen to fourteen and severe as less than eight, but its notes and the RCP 203 deck say thirteen to fifteen and eight or less.

36
00:04:21.973 --> 00:04:22.908
Use those.

37
00:04:24.333 --> 00:04:32.699
A score of eight or less means intubate, because these patients often cannot protect their airway.

38
00:04:32.699 --> 00:04:37.223
The deck calls this a guideline, not an absolute law.

39
00:04:37.223 --> 00:04:50.625
Chapter eleven adds that a score of eight or less is a reason to avoid CPAP to treat cardiogenic pulmonary edema; that is not a rule against CPAP in general.

40
00:04:50.625 --> 00:04:52.076
Then two caveats.

41
00:04:52.076 --> 00:05:00.100
An intubated patient cannot be tested on the verbal part, so the range becomes two T to ten T.

42
00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:06.758
And sedated or paralyzed patients may score low without a severe brain injury.

43
00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:10.212
Try the deck's example.

44
00:05:10.212 --> 00:05:18.748
A patient opens their eyes only to pain, makes incomprehensible sounds, and withdraws from pain.

45
00:05:18.748 --> 00:05:23.017
What is the Glasgow score, and how severe is it?

46
00:05:23.933 --> 00:05:29.933
[6-second practice pause]

47
00:05:30.433 --> 00:05:33.240
Eyes opening to pain scores two.

48
00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.223
Incomprehensible sounds score two.

49
00:05:36.223 --> 00:05:39.205
Withdrawing from pain scores four.

50
00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:44.293
Two plus two plus four is eight, which is severe, or coma.

51
00:05:44.293 --> 00:05:55.783
At eight or less these patients often cannot protect their airway, so intubation is indicated, as a guideline, not an absolute law.

52
00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:05.092
The Ballard method, used with the Dubowitz, estimates a newborn's gestational age after birth.

53
00:06:05.092 --> 00:06:14.328
It scores neuromuscular maturity and physical maturity, and the total corresponds to gestational age in weeks.

54
00:06:14.328 --> 00:06:18.358
It is particularly useful for premature infants.

55
00:06:18.358 --> 00:06:33.052
Respiratory therapists normally do not perform it, and you are not expected to perform or memorize it, only to know what it is, what it evaluates and what the score tells you.

56
00:06:33.052 --> 00:06:35.906
Hess adds the New Ballard details.

57
00:06:35.906 --> 00:06:39.769
A higher score means a higher gestational age.

58
00:06:39.769 --> 00:06:46.401
Forty equals forty weeks; below forty is preterm, and above forty is post-term.

59
00:06:46.401 --> 00:06:56.225
The scale runs from minus ten, or twenty weeks, to fifty, or forty four weeks, and each five points equals two weeks.

60
00:06:57.633 --> 00:07:04.618
No deck covers the Silverman score, so this comes from Hess chapter twenty two.

61
00:07:04.618 --> 00:07:08.065
It grades five signs, zero to two each.

62
00:07:08.065 --> 00:07:13.193
Upper chest: synchronized, lag on inspiration, or see-saw.

63
00:07:13.193 --> 00:07:18.055
Lower chest retractions: none, just visible, or marked.

64
00:07:18.055 --> 00:07:22.476
Xiphoid retraction: none, just visible, or marked.

65
00:07:22.476 --> 00:07:26.100
Nares dilation: none, minimal, or marked.

66
00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:33.438
Expiratory grunt: none, heard with a stethoscope only, or heard with the naked ear.

67
00:07:33.438 --> 00:07:37.593
The higher the score, the greater the distress.

68
00:07:37.593 --> 00:07:43.870
Grunting is the glottis closing to maintain lung volume, common in RDS.

69
00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:52.446
Retractions usually mean decreased chest compliance, and see-saw breathing means severe distress.

70
00:07:52.446 --> 00:08:03.408
Heuer's scale reads zero as no distress, one to six as mild to moderate, and seven or more as impending respiratory failure.

71
00:08:04.833 --> 00:08:05.685
Last one.

72
00:08:05.685 --> 00:08:09.846
A newborn has a New Ballard score of thirty.

73
00:08:09.846 --> 00:08:14.858
Is that a sign of illness, and what does it tell you?

74
00:08:15.767 --> 00:08:21.767
[6-second practice pause]

75
00:08:22.267 --> 00:08:22.867
No.

76
00:08:22.867 --> 00:08:28.396
Ballard is not a health score; it estimates gestational age.

77
00:08:28.396 --> 00:08:36.137
On the New Ballard, forty equals forty weeks, and each five points equals two weeks.

78
00:08:36.137 --> 00:08:43.509
Thirty is ten points below forty, so four weeks earlier: about thirty six weeks.

79
00:08:43.509 --> 00:08:45.905
Below forty means preterm.

80
00:08:45.905 --> 00:08:55.442
To judge how the baby is doing, use the Apgar right after birth, or the Silverman for respiratory distress.

81
00:08:56.867 --> 00:08:59.281
Here is the chapter in brief.

82
00:08:59.281 --> 00:09:06.357
The Apgar rates a newborn at one and five minutes, zero to ten, and higher is better.

83
00:09:06.357 --> 00:09:21.424
The Glasgow Coma Scale rates consciousness: eyes, verbal and motor, three to fifteen, with eight or less severe and a guideline to intubate; intubated patients score two T to ten T.

84
00:09:21.424 --> 00:09:28.584
The Ballard estimates gestational age, and on the New Ballard forty means forty weeks.

85
00:09:28.584 --> 00:09:36.992
The Silverman rates respiratory distress, zero to ten, and it runs the opposite way: higher is worse.
